'''Recursos del Marañón, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Recursos del Marañón}}), commonly known as '''RDM''', is a Balboan energy and minerals commodity trading company. Originally founded in 1938 in [[Port Soledat]], [[Potocsí]], the company relocated to [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]], in 1977 following the [[Camboriú War]] and the destruction of the Marañón basin's oil infrastructure. The company trades petroleum, bauxite, iron ore, and coal, sourcing from producers across Abyala and Fosia and selling to industrial buyers worldwide. It is dual-listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker RDM and on the [[Ampuria Stock Exchange]] in [[Volisania]], reflecting the company's significant trading relationships with Volisanian oil and minerals buyers.

RDM is the only Bolsa-listed company that originated outside Balboa and the only major commodity trader on the exchange focused on energy and minerals rather than agricultural products. The company's Potocsí origins and its relocation during the Camboriú War are central to its identity and its trading relationships in the Abyalan commodity markets.

== History ==

=== Founding in Potocsí (1938–1971) ===

Recursos del Marañón was founded in 1938 in [[Port Soledat]], [[Potocsí]], by [[Sebastià Solà i Vidal]], a Potocsino businessman from a Volisanian-descended family that had been involved in the Port Soledat shipping trade since the 19th century. The company was named after the [[Marañón basin]], the oil-rich region whose development was transforming the Potocsino economy in the early 20th century.

The company began as a private oil trading intermediary, buying crude oil from the independent producers operating in the Marañón basin — before the full nationalisation of the industry under [[PETROPOTO]] — and selling it to international buyers, primarily in Volisania and Breisland. Sebastià Solà's advantage was his family's shipping connections: the Solà family had operated vessels and warehousing in Port Soledat for two generations, and the company could manage the logistics of oil export as well as the trading.

In the 1950s and 1960s, RDM diversified beyond oil, adding bauxite, iron ore, and coal to its trading portfolio. The diversification was driven by the growth of the Potocsino mining sector, which was developing alongside the oil industry, and by Sebastià's recognition that a single-commodity trading house was excessively exposed to oil price volatility. The company established trading relationships with mining operations in the Vilcabamba highlands and the Llanos grasslands, and it began exporting bauxite and iron ore to industrial buyers in Volisania and Breisland.

By the late 1960s, RDM was one of the largest private commodity traders in Potocsí, with offices in Port Soledat, Ciutat Obrera, and Sant Llorenç, and a fleet of chartered vessels carrying oil and minerals to international ports. The company remained a family business, with Sebastià's sons joining the trade.

=== Military junta and the Camboriú War (1971–1977) ===

The military junta that seized power in Potocsí in 1971 placed increasing pressure on private commodity traders. The junta's economic policy favoured state control of the oil industry, and PETROPOTO's expansion into all aspects of petroleum production and trading squeezed the private intermediaries. The Solà family, which had maintained good relations with the pre-junta civilian governments, found itself marginalised by the new regime.

The [[Camboriú War]] (1976–1977) was the decisive event. The war between the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] and Potocsí over the Marañón basin produced the [[Marañón oil fires]], which caused an estimated $4.5 billion in damage to Potocsino oil infrastructure. RDM's Port Soledat offices and warehousing were damaged in the fighting, and the company's trading relationships with the Marañón basin producers were disrupted as the oil fields were set ablaze.

[[Sebastià Solà i Vidal]] died in 1976, shortly before the war began, and the company was inherited by his son [[Ricard Solà i Masdeu]], who made the decision to relocate the company to Balboa. The relocation was driven by three factors: the physical destruction of the company's Port Soledat operations, the junta's increasing hostility to private enterprise, and the recognition that the post-war Potocsino oil industry would be dominated by PETROPOTO, leaving little room for private traders.

=== Relocation to Balboa (1977) ===

In 1977, RDM relocated its headquarters to [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]. The choice of Balboa was strategic: the [[Balboa Canal]] provided access to both the Abyalan and Fosian markets, the country's political stability and neutral foreign policy provided a safe base for international trading, and the Solà family's Volisanian heritage made the cultural transition natural.

The relocation was difficult. The company had lost most of its physical assets in Potocsí, and its trading relationships with the Marañón basin producers were severed by the war. Ricard Solà rebuilt the business from a small office in the Districte Financer, initially focusing on trading Potocsino oil through intermediaries — buying from PETROPOTO and selling to international buyers — and gradually re-establishing the mineral trading operations that had been less affected by the war.

The Balboan government, which was seeking to develop the country's financial and trading sector, welcomed RDM's relocation and provided assistance with regulatory approvals and banking relationships. The company was incorporated as a Balboan entity in 1978 and was granted a trading licence by the Ministry of Trade.

=== Rebuilding and growth (1977–2000) ===

The 1980s and 1990s were a period of rebuilding and growth. The restoration of diplomatic relations between Balisca and Potocsí in 1984, and the democratic transition in Potocsí in 1985, allowed RDM to re-establish direct trading relationships with Potocsino producers. The company opened an office in Port Soledat in 1986, its first permanent presence in Potocsí since the relocation.

The company also expanded its sourcing beyond Potocsí, establishing trading relationships with mining operations in [[Tapuya]], [[Asikyira]], and [[Lacashe]], and with oil producers in the Salsipuedes Sea region. The diversification of sourcing reduced the company's dependence on the Potocsino market and positioned it as a pan-Abyalan commodity trader rather than a Potocsino exile company.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] and the [[Ampuria Stock Exchange]] in 1996, with the Solà family retaining a 40 per cent stake. The dual listing reflected the company's strong trading ties with Volisania, which was the largest market for both its oil and minerals trading. The listing raised capital that was used to expand the company's trading operations and to develop its commodity hedging business. The [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] and the [[1994 Balboa spot recession]] had a limited impact on RDM, as the company's business was in international commodity trading rather than the domestic Balboan market.

=== Modern era (2000–present) ===

The 2000s and 2010s saw RDM grow into a major energy and minerals trader, with a trading portfolio that spanned oil, bauxite, iron ore, and coal, and a geographic reach that extended from Abyala to Fosia. The company's oil trading business benefited from the rising oil prices of the 2000s, and its minerals trading business grew as the Fosian industrial demand for bauxite and iron ore expanded.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 produced a sharp but temporary decline in oil prices, as global travel restrictions reduced demand for petroleum. RDM's oil trading desk reported losses in the first half of 2020, but the minerals trading business — particularly iron ore and coal — remained strong, as industrial demand in Fosia recovered quickly. The company's diversified portfolio, which had been a source of complexity in the early years, proved to be its principal strength during the pandemic.

By 2026, RDM employed approximately 4,200 people and operated trading offices in Sant Cristòfor, Port Soledat, Volisania, Tapuya, Sinchew, Zong, Okaiken, and Lacashe. The Solà family retained a 26 per cent stake, and the company was the second-largest commodity trader on the Bolsa, after [[Més Gran Corporació]].

== Operations ==

RDM operates across four principal commodity divisions:

* '''Oil trading''': The company's original and largest business, trading crude oil and refined petroleum products sourced primarily from Potocsí and the Salsipuedes Sea region. The oil trading desk manages physical cargo flows, freight contracts, and price hedging for the company's own account and for producer clients. The company is one of the largest private oil traders in the Abyalan market.
* '''Bauxite trading''': The company trades bauxite sourced from mining operations in the Vilcabamba highlands of Potocsí and from Tapuya, selling to aluminium smelters in Volisania, Sinchew, and Okaiken. The bauxite trading desk was established in the 1950s and has grown significantly as Fosian industrial demand for aluminium has expanded.
* '''Iron ore trading''': The company trades iron ore sourced from mining operations in Potocsí and Asikyira, selling to steel producers in Fosia and Abyala. The iron ore trading desk was established in the 1960s and is the company's second-largest division by revenue after oil.
* '''Coal trading''': The company trades metallurgical and thermal coal sourced from Asikyira and Lacashe, selling to steel producers and power generators in Abyala and Fosia. The coal trading desk is the company's smallest division but has grown as Fosian industrial demand has expanded.

The company's headquarters are at 160 Carrer Frederic, in the Districte Financer of Sant Cristòfor, and it maintains international trading offices in Port Soledat, Volisania, Tapuya, Sinchew, Zong, Okaiken, and Lacashe.

